=== objections ===
==== knowledge of sentence synonymy taken for granted ====
That sentential synonymy is discernible is not a thesis of the _Tractatus_ but
rather an unquestioned assumption.

== classes and properties ==



== the world of the happy man ==
    6.43	If the good or bad exercise of the will does alter the world, it can
        alter only the limits of the world, not the facts—not what can be
        expressed by means of language.  In short the effect must be that it
        becomes an altogether different world. It must, so to speak, wax and
        wane as a whole.  The world of the happy man is a different one from
        that of the unhappy man.

Is it remotely plausible that the world of the happy man is a different world
from that of the unhappy man? Is there some metaphorical sense in which we can
redeem it? The range of possibilities that the unhappy man dwells on are likely
different from those a happy man focuses on.

== basis of logical space ==
Another aspect of W's approach that must be mentioned is the notion of finding a
logical basis for the space of grammatically correct statements. This is
directly analogous to the notion of a basis of a mathematical vector space.
There the notion is that any vector in the space (mutatis mutandis any point in
the space) can be reached/expressed/constructed as a combination((sum of multiples))
of the members of the basis. 



