surprised: My reaction after a first look is that this confirms my view.
Consider the first line: “Can a form be defined? Can e.g. the subject-predicate
form be defined as the class of all subject-predicate propositions?”

See that here he is talking about exactly the move I was describing above (quite
heavy-handedly 😦 ) As soon as he wishes to define a "form" (i.e. a predicate
true of the class of propositions with that form) the immediate suggestion is to
define it "as" the class of propositions with that form. The identity between a
property and the class of objects with that property is the very first thing he
considers. So I take this as confirmation that he is thinking about the
technique.

“Yes”, I imagine you saying @Fafner, “but he goes on directly to _*reject*_
that view. Hence your (surprised's) account is mistaken.”

W rejects it on the grounds that this quasi-identity is something that can only
be shown and not said: “If a form were definable, we could not understand it
without a definition. But the possibility of expressing a sense rests on the
very fact that we understand a form without its being explained to us. A
proposition shows its form.”

Let me make it clear that in my explication of what is going on in W I pay no
heed to the show/say distinction which W is so attached to. My account flowed
from a discussion of the problems of show/say with @Neg F∀lsum and I made it
clear that I was describing Ws semantics without pretending to obey the show/say
distinction and just describing Ws semantic theory directly (i.e. saying the
theory directly) without pretense.

I take W to be an outright hypocrite on this point: it is no good to say
something and then take it back saying
    6.54 My propositions serve as elucidations in the following way: anyone who
    understands me eventually recognizes them as nonsensical, when he has used
    them—as steps—to climb up beyond them. (He must, so to speak, throw away the
    ladder after he has climbed up it.) He must transcend these propositions,
    and then he will see the world aright.
Nonsense is nonsense. If it says nothing about meaning it cannot elucidate it
either. Once again, if it can't be said it can't be whistled!

Hence I take W's view to be precisely the one I described, only with an added
(and misplaced) doctrine that it cannot strictly be said but only shown.

Or to put it another way, if you wish to take the say/show distinction
seriously: My claims about the identity of semantic features and the classes of
propositions having those features "serve as elucidations in the following way:
anyone who understands me (surprised) eventually recognises them as
nonsensical". It is a mere attempt to say what can only be shown, and once you
see what I am showing you can "transcend my claims and see the world aright"
having discarded my claims as, strictly speaking, nonsense.

[It turns out I can whistle too!!] 

For further confirmation of my view see W's comments on numbers: he wishes to
claim that the same relationship between 3 and collections of three things (i.e.
the exemplification of a form) obtains between propositional forms and
particular propositions. In this case Russell's construction of numbers as the
set of sets with that number of members is precisely an exemplification of the
technique I described. W's is simply the unsayable showable only version of that
technique. (strip out sets and insert the word "form" if you like - even in
talking about forms W is again breaching his own proscriptions against saying
what can only be shown!!) 
